SUMMARY:WAVES VIENNA: DAS SCHOTTISCHE PRINZIP (AT) / KOMBYNAT ROBOTRON (DE) / MORN (UK) / WHY HORSES? (UK) (Uhrzeit prüfen)
LOCATION:Chelsea
DESCRIPTION:Doors: 18:15   WHY HORSES? (UK) – 18:30 EST? 2022  Origin? Cardiff Likes? Kurt Vonnegut  Hates? when all songs sound the same FFO? Iceage (the band)\, Brazil (the 1985 ?lm)\, and Modest Mouse  Why Horses? are comprised of Will Dadswell (Violin)\, Joel Hadi Checri (Drums)\, Dilibe Aneke (Guitar)\, Les Davies (Bass) and Gabriel Austin Lester (Guitar/Vox). They make post-punk and art rock inspired music combined with cryptic lyricism which they call  ‘Post Hypnagogic Punk Wave.’ In October\, they embark on their ?rst headline tour across the UK\, bringing with them new songs\, stories\, and friendship. www.instagram.com/whyhorses_/    MORN (UK) – 19:45 Since landing in London in 2024\, MORN have quickly embedded themselves in the capital’s DIY underground\, selling out headline shows across London including feral\, full-throttle sets at The Windmill Brixton\, closing out Independent Venue Week. They also saw themselves included in Glastonbury’s Emerging Talent longlist. MORN are a striking new presence emerging from the farmlands of Monmouth\, South Wales. Two sets of siblings crafting a compelling blend of what they refer to as “doom over beautiful chords.” Announcing their arrival with the breathless\, runaway train chaoticism of ‘Modern Man’\, MORN weave spiky guitar passages and rumbling fuzz\, with an urgency that feels as though they can’t get to the end of the song fast enough. With its nihilistic gang vocal of “Life is sh*t when you’re all alone\, but it’s ?ne”\, and its spiral into an unhinged closing chorus of “La la la”s\, MORN are marking themselves out early as a dextrous band that are happy wrestling bedlam into something cathartic. ‘Modern Man’ is a thrilling introduction to a band just getting started. www.instagram.com/morn.band/?hl=de    KOMBYNAT ROBOTRON (DE) - 21:00 Towering walls of sound are built up and torn apart as repetitive\, fuzz-drenched bass patterns and soaring drums create the foundation for swirling guitar solos and explosive noise-rock eruptions\, from which heavily processed vocals occasionally emerge. Kombynat Robotron create a hypnotic sonic universe where the boundaries between krautrock\, noise\, psychedelia and punk blur and disappear. Their latest album AANK\, released via FUZZ CLUB in summer 2025 and celebrated by critics and fans alike\, showcases a band that continues to evolve on its own terms while staying true to its musical roots. Whether performing at international festivals or in small\, smoke-?lled clubs\, their live shows are immersive sonic journeys balancing on the ?ne line between chaos and a fragile sense of control. We’re ?ying to the stars. Robotron over. www.instagram.com/kombynat_robotron/    DAS SCHOTTISCHE PRINZIP (AT) – 22:15 Das Schottische Prinzip consists of Julia Reißner (vocals\, guitar\, keys)\, Jana Mitrovic (bass)\, Viktoria Mezovsky (guitar) and Jennifer Gitschner (drums). Their “atomic priesthood” references science-?ction ideas of preserving knowledge across generations — here\, the knowledge and warning of what it means to be human. At the centre is Julia Reißner\, raised with military discipline in Lower Austria and the USA. After an unnecessary philosophy degree\, she taught herself music on YouTube using a badly out-of-tune piano that mysteriously ended up in her basement. Her work combines intellectual sharpness\, rebelliousness and artistic individuality. www.instagram.com/dasschottischeprinzip/?hl=de  www.wavesvienna.com/  Tickets: www.universe.com/events/waves-vienna-2026-tickets-3KLWXG
